  "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: Officials never wrote of soft power; the phrase appears in no volume of the series. They wrote of information programs, psychological strategy and the American image. They planned messages to show sympathy for Asian nationalism and to expose Soviet imperialism, believing that Asians would back whoever seemed to be winning. They judged their own results guardedly, from a Tokyo committee that had not been effective to a director who found efforts at unity in South Vietnam had not progressed to his satisfaction. Their recurring conclusion was that Asian audiences judged America by its deeds, its commitments and its staying power more than by its words. Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came from the records of the U.S. Information Agency, the Department of State's central decimal file and its lot files on National Security Council papers, and the Johnson, Kennedy, Nixon, Ford and Carter presidential libraries; the footnotes cite Department of State lots 66 D 95, 62 D 430, 63 D 351 and 62 D 1 and library files that FRUS does not print. What this request cannot get from FRUS: any measurement of how Asian audiences received the messages, the messages themselves, and Asian voices other than through American summaries. The Agency's research reports, its Vietnam working group files and the Ford Library's Vietnam Information Group files hold more.",
